@page "/"
@page "/movies"
@implements IDisposable
@inject CatalogService Catalog
@inject Announcer Announcer
@inject QueryState Address
@inject TimeProvider Time
@inject IJSRuntime Js

@* The films page. Task 6 replaces the body of the Ready state with the tile grid and KEEPS these states.

   The PageTitle and the h1 are in the first render and outside every state branch: FocusOnNavigate fires
   once after the first render and never retries, and movies.json arrives long after that. *@
<PageTitle>Films - Film and TV library</PageTitle>

@* tabindex="-1" is not for FocusOnNavigate, which adds it itself: focusBySelector reads
   `if (!element.hasAttribute('tabindex')) { element.tabIndex = -1 }` before focusing. It is for
   ElementReference.FocusAsync(), which does NOT add it and on an element that cannot take focus fails
   silently -- no exception, no moved focus, no failing test. The successful-retry transition below depends
   on this attribute, so it is a feature dependency here and not a conformance claim. *@
<h1 tabindex="-1" @ref="heading">Films</h1>

@* ONE stable container, not three sibling branches that swap nodes: replacing the node would destroy the
   node a screen reader's virtual cursor may be sitting on. The paragraph is the same element in every
   state and only its text changes; the button is the only thing that comes and goes.

   No live region of any kind in here: no alert role, no status role, no log role, and no live-region
   attribute. (Spelled in words on purpose: SourceContractTests scans these files for the literal markers, and a
   comment quoting one fails exactly as the attribute would.) The announcer is the only speaking
   channel -- a region here would be a second utterance from a second assertive source, racing the hidden
   one in an order nobody controls -- and the deterministic channel for the failure is the focus move, not
   a region. SC 4.1.3 Status Messages, Level AA, applies only to messages that do NOT receive focus. *@
<div class="catalog-state" tabindex="-1" @ref="stateContainer">
    <p>@Sentence</p>

    @if (ShowTryAgain)
    {
        @* aria-disabled, never the disabled ATTRIBUTE: disabling the focused element blurs it and drops
           focus to <body>. The click still arrives and is ignored in TryAgain instead. *@
        <button type="button" class="try-again" aria-disabled="@(retrying ? "true" : null)" @onclick="TryAgain">Try again</button>
    }
</div>

@* THE FILTERS AND THE GRID, AND ONLY IN THE READY STATE. Nothing else in this file's markup is Task 8's to
   change: the title, the heading and the one stable state container all stay exactly as Task 5 left them.
   The page owns the filter and the page number and hands the grid the FILTERED list.
   It sits BELOW the state container, so the reading order is the heading, then the one-sentence state, then
   the filters, then the count, then the pager, then the tiles -- and the pager is above the tiles rather than
   below them so that paging does not leave the viewport at the tail of the new page.

   The panel is not offered over an empty library: there is nothing to filter, and Task 5's own sentence has
   already said so. The same condition keeps the no-match paragraph below from ever being reached by one --
   "No titles match these filters." over a library with nothing in it would be false twice over.

   PAGE SIZE IS NOT PASSED. The grid's default is 60, which makes 25 pages of the measured 1,456 records, and
   a second copy of the number here would be one edit away from disagreeing with the first. *@
@if (ShowFilters)
{
    @* THE TWO-COLUMN WRAPPER, and both divs earn their place. The outer one is the grid; the inner one
       exists because PosterGrid renders TWO roots (.grid-head and ul.grid), so without it auto-placement
       would put the head in the second column and the tiles back in the first.

       A PLAIN DIV AND NEVER <aside>, <nav> OR <section>. FacetPanel's root is already a <search> landmark
       and the whole-panel-is-one-landmark ruling depends on it; an <aside> inside <main> maps to
       complementary (it degrades to generic only inside article/aside/nav/section, and main is none of
       those), which would nest a search landmark inside a complementary one and file the filters under
       Complementary in a landmark list. A div is invisible to assistive technology, which is the point.

       DOM ORDER IS UNCHANGED AND THE PANEL RENDERS LEFT. Facets first in the DOM, painted in the first
       column, reads the same way in either order, so SC 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence has nothing to catch.
       Painting the panel on the RIGHT while it stayed first in the DOM is what would engage 1.3.2 and
       SC 2.4.3 Focus Order with it -- a sighted reader meeting results-then-filters while a keyboard
       reader tabs filters-then-results -- so there is no order:, no row-reverse and no grid-template-areas
       here. The reading order this file rules on above, the ClearAll focus return, and PosterGrid's
       pager-above-tiles argument all depend on this order too. *@
    <div class="movies-layout">
        <FacetPanel @ref="panel" State="Effective" TypedQuery="@typedQuery" Genres="genreOptions"
                    Decades="decadeOptions" HasUnknownYear="anyYearUnknown"
                    QueryInput="OnQueryInput" StateChanged="OnFacetChanged" />

        <div class="movies-results">

        @* Zero results is TEXT, not an empty grid. The grid's whole head -- count, pager and tiles -- is gated on
           having titles, so filtering to nothing removes the pager from the DOM, and a pager button that held focus
           takes it with it: the next Tab would restart from the top of the document. This paragraph is where focus
           goes when that happens (see OnAfterRenderAsync), and tabindex="-1" is a feature dependency here: the
           focus call does not add it and fails silently on an element that cannot take focus. *@
        @if (shown.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="no-match">
                <p tabindex="-1" @ref="emptyMessage">@NoMatch</p>
                <button type="button" class="clear-filters" @onclick="ClearAll">Clear all filters</button>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        @* A METHOD GROUP AND NOT A LAMBDA for KeyOf. "film => film.K" would put a > inside an attribute
           value, and SourceContractTests reads this tag with a regex that stops at the first > -- so the
           contract would see half the tag and report attributes the page does not pass. A named method also
           says in one place what identifies a film, which is K and never I. *@
        <PosterGrid TItem="MovieRecord" Items="shown" KeyOf="PublishedKey"
                    Page="page" PageChanged="OnPageChanged" Context="film">
            <PosterTile Tile="TileView.ForFilm(film)" />
        </PosterGrid>
    }
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The failure sentence. ONE constant for the visible paragraph and for the <c>Alert</c>, so the seen and
    /// the spoken copies cannot drift apart. Internal so a test can assert they are the same string rather
    /// than two literals that happen to match today.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string CouldNotLoad =
        "The film library could not be loaded. Check your connection and try again.";

    /// <summary>The visible loading copy, with the ellipsis CHARACTER rather than three full stops.</summary>
    /// <summary>
    /// What identifies one film to the grid's <c>@key</c>: the published key, never the TMDB id.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>I</c> is 0 for every unmatched film, and a duplicate key within one collection fails the render.
    /// <c>K</c>'s distinctness across the whole catalogue is pinned by the enrich pipeline's own tests, on
    /// the published json's <c>k</c> field, which is the artifact the browser actually reads.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string PublishedKey(MovieRecord film) => film.K;

    internal const string LoadingVisible = "Loading films…";

    /// <summary>
    /// The spoken loading copy. It differs from the visible one in punctuation only, which is deliberate and
    /// is not drift: an ellipsis is noise read aloud.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string LoadingSpoken = "Loading films";

    /// <summary>
    /// A successful fetch of nothing gets its own sentence. It must never reach Task 8's "No titles match
    /// these filters.", which would be false: no filter is involved, and nothing is being hidden.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string EmptyLibrary = "The library is empty.";

    /// <summary>What the page says, seen and spoken, when the filters leave nothing. One constant for both copies.</summary>
    internal const string NoMatch = "No titles match these filters.";

    // Filter at ~150ms so the grid feels immediate; announce at ~750ms so a screen reader user hears
    // "174 titles match." once rather than a stream of interruptions per keystroke. The gap between the two is
    // the point: SC 4.1.3 Status Messages is the criterion a filtering UI most often fails, and the announcer
    // restarts its own settle delay on every call, so a caller that spoke per keystroke would deliver nothing.
    internal const int FilterDebounceMs = 150;
    internal const int AnnounceDebounceMs = 750;

    // The address is the page's state, read the router's way. Every one is a STRING so that a value the router
    // cannot convert is junk to ignore and not an unhandled exception; QueryState does the parsing.
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = QueryState.QueryKey)] public string? Q { get; set; }

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = QueryState.ConfidenceKey)] public string? Conf { get; set; }

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = QueryState.GenreKey)] public string[]? Genre { get; set; }

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = QueryState.DecadeKey)] public string[]? Decade { get; set; }

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = QueryState.SortKey)] public string? SortParam { get; set; }

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = QueryState.PageKey)] public string? PageParam { get; set; }

    private ElementReference heading;
    private ElementReference stateContainer;

    // Explicitly Loading with an EMPTY list, never default(CatalogResult<>): that default pairs Loading with
    // a null value, and the first render would throw on Value.Count.
    private CatalogResult<IReadOnlyList<MovieRecord>> films = new(CatalogState.Loading, []);

    private bool disposed;
    private bool retrying;
    private bool loadingAnnounced;

    // True once THIS failure has been reported to a screen reader, by whichever channel did it: the focus move
    // or the alert. It is not the same thing as movedFocusToFailure, and the difference is the whole of the
    // ordering below. movedFocusToFailure is set only when a move actually happened, so on every later render
    // of the same failure "no move happened on this pass" is true again -- and keying the alert off that alone
    // would raise it one render late, which moves the double speech rather than removing it. Reset on a retry,
    // because a second failure is a second thing the user needs telling about (criterion 8).
    private bool failureReported;
    private bool movedFocusToFailure;

    // True only while a focus call is suspended at its await. A render can arrive in that window -- the
    // announcer delivers a message from a timer callback, MainLayout re-renders on it, and this page
    // re-renders with it -- and THAT render's OnAfterRenderAsync would see movedFocusToFailure still false
    // and issue a second focus call, so the page would fight itself for focus.
    // Honest history: the symptom that first suggested this guard turned out to be a test-harness artefact,
    // and with the guard neutralised every test still passed -- which made it unfalsifiable code, the one
    // thing rule T1 forbids shipping. It is kept because the window is real, and it is kept honest by
    // MoviesStateTests.ARenderArrivingWhileTheFocusCallIsInFlightDoesNotIssueASecondOne, which holds the
    // interop call open and renders into it. Neutralise this condition and that test fails.
    // It has a second job since the failure report was reordered: a render arriving in that window must not
    // decide that no focus move happened and raise the alert, because the suspended pass has not finished
    // deciding. See the Failed arm.
    private bool movingFocus;
    private bool focusHeadingAfterRetry;
    private bool countAnnounced;

    /// <summary>
    /// The page of tiles on show, one-based. The PAGE owns this and not the grid: the grid raises
    /// <c>PageChanged</c> and this adopts it, UNCHANGED (see <see cref="OnPageChanged"/>) -- the grid builds its
    /// announcement from the number it raised, so a page that clamped or ignored it would have the grid state a
    /// page the visitor is not on. It is also how the grid corrects a value out of range: it renders the
    /// clamped page and raises the clamped number for this to adopt.
    /// </summary>
    private int page = 1;

    /// <summary>
    /// True from the moment <see cref="AdoptAddress"/> takes a page from the address until the render that hands
    /// that page to the grid has finished. The grid answers a page out of range with a correction, raised through
    /// the same <c>PageChanged</c> a button press uses and inside that very render, so this is the only thing that
    /// tells the two apart. See <see cref="OnPageChanged"/>.
    /// </summary>
    private bool awaitingGridCorrection;

    // ---- the filter. The ADDRESS is the state; these are the page's working copy of it, adopted eagerly so a
    // choice shows at once and reconciled against what the router hands back (see AdoptAddress).

    /// <summary>The filter the list is showing, as the address says it. Raw: Effective is what can be displayed.</summary>
    private FilterState filter = FilterState.Default;

    /// <summary>What the search box shows. Ahead of <see cref="filter"/> by up to the filter debounce.</summary>
    private string typedQuery = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// The canonical text of the last state this page wrote to the address or adopted from it. When the router
    /// hands back an address with this same text it is the echo of our own navigation and is ignored; anything
    /// else is the visitor following a link, and replaces the working copy. Comparing STATES rather than strings
    /// of the address is what makes a normalised junk value (<c>?page=0</c>) read as the same thing it was
    /// normalised to.
    /// </summary>
    private string? syncedWith;

    private CatalogMeta meta = CatalogMeta.Empty;
    private IReadOnlyList<GenreOption> genreOptions = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<int> decadeOptions = [];
    private bool anyYearUnknown;

    /// <summary>The films that pass the filter, in order. What the grid is given.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<MovieRecord> shown = [];

    private FacetPanel? panel;
    private ElementReference emptyMessage;

    /// <summary>True once focus has been dealt with for THIS run of zero results; reset when there are some again.</summary>
    private bool emptyHandled;

    private ITimer? filterTimer;
    private ITimer? announceTimer;

    /// <summary>
    /// The filter as the panel can show it. Choices the catalogue gives it no control for -- a genre the meta does
    /// not name, a decade nobody is in -- are dropped, so a filter the visitor can neither see nor untick is not
    /// silently narrowing the list.
    /// </summary>
    private FilterState Effective => filter.Within(genreOptions.Select(g => g.Id), decadeOptions, anyYearUnknown);

    private bool ShowFilters => films.State == CatalogState.Ready && films.Value.Count > 0;

    /// <summary>
    /// The button stays while a retry is in flight. Removing it would blur the element the user is standing
    /// on and drop focus to <c>&lt;body&gt;</c>, which is the same defect as using the disabled attribute.
    /// </summary>
    private bool ShowTryAgain => films.State == CatalogState.Failed || retrying;

    /// <summary>
    /// What is said when the page first reaches Ready. The same words that are on screen -- unless the address
    /// arrived with a filter already in it, when the library's total is not what the visitor is looking at and
    /// the count of what they are looking at is.
    /// </summary>
    private string OpeningSentence => ShowFilters && Effective.IsFiltering ? CountSentence(shown.Count) : Sentence;

    /// <summary>
    /// The one sentence on screen, per state. No count while Loading or Failed: a count is a claim about the
    /// library, and neither of those states knows anything about the library.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// THE TOTAL FOLLOWS THE BROWSER'S CULTURE, and that is the ruling rather than an oversight. This call
    /// passed CultureInfo.InvariantCulture while PosterGrid's head does not, so a de-DE visitor read "The
    /// library holds 1,456 films." two lines above "Showing 1-60 of 1.456 titles." -- one number, two
    /// groupings, in one block of text. The brief's M6 already decided that Blazor WebAssembly taking its
    /// culture from the browser is correct behaviour and not a bug to stamp out, and acceptance criterion
    /// 11 requires 1.456 under de-DE with a test behind it, so making the grid invariant would have
    /// contradicted a shipped criterion. This was the half to change.
    /// No existing assertion moved with it: every other page-sentence test in the suite sits at 1, 2 or 61
    /// films, none of which has a group separator in any culture, which is exactly why the behaviour needed
    /// MoviesGridTests.TheSentenceAndTheGridHeadGroupTheSameNumberTheSameWay rather than a silent edit.
    /// </remarks>
    private string Sentence => films.State switch
    {
        CatalogState.Failed => CouldNotLoad,
        CatalogState.Loading => LoadingVisible,
        _ => films.Value.Count switch
        {
            0 => EmptyLibrary,
            1 => "The library holds 1 film.",
            var n => $"The library holds {n.ToString("N0")} films.",
        },
    };

    /// <remarks>
    /// Nothing is announced here. Criterion 5 and rule R4: a message raised before the first render starts
    /// its settle timer while the clearing render is still queued, so the real gap becomes "delay minus
    /// handler time" and collapses on exactly the slow devices the gap exists for. Everything this page says
    /// it says from <c>OnAfterRenderAsync</c>.
    /// <para>
    /// The fetch is awaited and never cancelled, here or on dispose. The task is shared and cached by the
    /// service, so cancelling it would turn one user's navigation away into a Failed result for the next
    /// visit to this page.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var result = await FetchAsync();
        if (disposed) return;

        films = result;
        if (result.State == CatalogState.Ready) IndexCatalogue();
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Before the first await, so the state the address asks for is in place before the catalogue arrives and
    /// the first Ready render shows the filtered list rather than the whole library for one frame.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override void OnInitialized() => AdoptAddress();

    /// <remarks>
    /// Runs on EVERY set of parameters, which includes the router handing the page the address it just wrote.
    /// See <see cref="AdoptAddress"/>.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override void OnParametersSet() => AdoptAddress();

    /// <summary>
    /// Both documents, in parallel; the catalogue decides the outcome. The genre names are only needed when the
    /// films are, and a meta that failed is <c>CatalogMeta.Empty</c> -- no genres, no genre fieldset, every other
    /// facet intact -- so its state is deliberately not looked at: Task 5 owns the failure sentence and a second
    /// one here would be a second voice for the same fetch.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task<CatalogResult<IReadOnlyList<MovieRecord>>> FetchAsync()
    {
        var moviesRequest = Catalog.GetMoviesAsync();
        var metaRequest = Catalog.GetMetaAsync();

        var result = await moviesRequest;
        if (result.State == CatalogState.Ready) meta = (await metaRequest).Value;

        return result;
    }

    /// <summary>Builds what the panel offers from the catalogue, and filters it for the first time.</summary>
    private void IndexCatalogue()
    {
        genreOptions = CatalogFilter.GenreOptions(meta);
        decadeOptions = CatalogFilter.DecadesOf(films.Value);
        anyYearUnknown = CatalogFilter.AnyYearUnknown(films.Value);
        Refilter();
    }

    private void Refilter() => shown = CatalogFilter.Apply(films.Value, Effective);

    /// <remarks>
    /// Every announcement and every focus move, after the render that carries the new state.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        // First, before any await: this must be the render's own call, and not a continuation of an earlier one.
        awaitingGridCorrection = false;

        if (disposed) return;

        switch (films.State)
        {
            case CatalogState.Loading:
                // Criterion 6: only because the page is STILL loading after a render, and at most once a
                // visit. On a return visit the catalogue is cached, the task is already complete,
                // ComponentBase produces no intermediate render and the page is Ready on the first render --
                // so this never runs, and nothing is ever said about a page that had already loaded. A load
                // fast enough to finish inside the settle delay says nothing either: the count supersedes
                // this message in the same region before it is ever delivered.
                if (!loadingAnnounced)
                {
                    loadingAnnounced = true;
                    Announcer.Status(LoadingSpoken);
                }

                break;

            case CatalogState.Failed:
                // R7: this page armed the polite region with the loading line, so this page clears it. The
                // failure is carried by the focus move or the alert below, and a region still saying
                // "Loading films" beside a page that says the library could not be loaded is a stale statement.
                // Idempotent over an already-empty region.
                Announcer.Status("");

                // Criterion 7, the first transition: once, and only if focus is still where the page put it.
                // THE FOCUS MOVE IS THE PRIMARY CHANNEL and it runs FIRST, because a screen reader reads the
                // contents of a focused unnamed tabindex="-1" container and this container's whole text IS the
                // failure sentence. A move that happens therefore already says it, in the words on screen, and
                // R3 forbids announcing the text of whatever focus has just moved to.
                // The flag is set only when the move actually happened, so a user who had the drawer open when
                // the fetch failed is not denied the move for ever.
                var movedOnThisPass = false;
                if (!movedFocusToFailure && !movingFocus)
                {
                    movingFocus = true;
                    try { movedOnThisPass = movedFocusToFailure = await FocusIfUnmoved(stateContainer); }
                    finally { movingFocus = false; }
                }

                // R11: one report for one failure the user can see, and THE ALERT IS THE FALLBACK -- raised
                // only where the focus move did not happen. Raising both speaks the sentence twice: once as the
                // contents of the container focus landed on, and again a settle delay later when the assertive
                // region interrupts with the identical text.
                // On a FAILED RETRY focus is usually already on the Try again button, which is not a keeper, so
                // the helper refuses, nothing moves, and this fires -- which is how criterion 8's promise that a
                // user who retries and fails again is not met with silence survives the reordering. "Usually" is
                // doing work in that sentence; see the remarks on PageFocus.Keepers.
                // Raised here and NEVER from the click handler, so the clear that handler wrote has reached the
                // DOM first and the announcer can anchor its settle delay to that render. Without the gap a
                // repeated identical sentence is no DOM change at all.
                // movingFocus is in the condition because a render can arrive while the first pass's focus call
                // is suspended at its await: that pass has not chosen a channel yet, and this one must not
                // choose for it. It is also the last statement in this arm, which is what R4 asks for.
                if (!failureReported && !movingFocus)
                {
                    failureReported = true;
                    if (!movedOnThisPass) Announcer.Alert(CouldNotLoad);
                }

                break;

            case CatalogState.Ready:
                // Criterion 7, the third transition, and the order is the point. Focus first: landing on the
                // h1 announces "Films, heading level 1", which does not tell the user the retry worked, and
                // R3 forbids announcing the text of whatever focus has just moved to. The count therefore
                // follows as its own statement rather than being read off the heading.
                if (focusHeadingAfterRetry)
                {
                    focusHeadingAfterRetry = false;
                    await FocusOutright(heading);
                }

                // The opening announcement is CLAIMED before any await below, so a render that arrives while a
                // focus call is suspended cannot make it a second time.
                var opening = !countAnnounced;
                countAnnounced = true;

                // Zero results, once per run of them. The grid's whole head is gated on having titles, so
                // filtering to nothing removes the pager, and a pager button that held focus takes it with it
                // (rule R9). The move goes through the same guard the failed state uses: it happens only if
                // focus is nowhere in particular (<body>, which is where a removed button leaves it) or where
                // the page itself put it. A visitor typing in the search box is NOT moved: stealing focus from a
                // field mid-word is worse than the thing it would fix, and they hear the count instead.
                var movedToEmpty = false;
                if (ShowFilters && shown.Count == 0)
                {
                    if (!emptyHandled)
                    {
                        emptyHandled = true;
                        movedToEmpty = await FocusIfUnmoved(emptyMessage);

                        // R3: the paragraph focus landed on is read aloud as it takes focus, so announcing
                        // the same words as well would be two statements for one event.
                        if (movedToEmpty) Stop(announceTimer);
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    emptyHandled = false;
                }

                if (opening && !movedToEmpty)
                {
                    Announcer.Status(OpeningSentence);
                }

                break;
        }
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Nothing auto-retries and nothing auto-dismisses. The failed text is a state, not a toast: a message
    /// that cleared itself would be a content-set time limit on reading it, SC 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable,
    /// Level A, and an auto-retry with a visible countdown would engage the same criterion.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task TryAgain()
    {
        // The button carries aria-disabled rather than the disabled attribute, so the click still arrives
        // while a retry is in flight and has to be ignored here.
        if (retrying) return;

        // R5: the failure has ended as far as the user is concerned. A cancellation, not a message -- it
        // takes effect at once and speaks nothing. The next Alert is raised from OnAfterRenderAsync and
        // never in this handler: on a device slow enough to flush both mutations in one frame the second
        // call would see the region already empty, mark the clear observed, and the repeated identical
        // sentence would reach the DOM as no change at all.
        Announcer.Alert("");

        retrying = true;
        failureReported = false;
        movedFocusToFailure = false;
        films = new(CatalogState.Loading, films.Value);   // criterion 8: the loading copy, visibly

        var result = await FetchAsync();
        if (disposed) return;

        retrying = false;
        films = result;
        if (result.State == CatalogState.Ready) IndexCatalogue();
        focusHeadingAfterRetry = result.State == CatalogState.Ready;
    }

    // ============================================================ saying how many

    /// <summary>
    /// The count, once, after the visitor has stopped changing things. The COUNT ONLY: the grid announces its own
    /// page changes through the same region, so a page sentence from here as well would be two statements racing
    /// for one region and the visitor would hear one of them, unpredictably.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Armed only after the opening announcement has been made, which says its own sentence.
    /// </remarks>
    private void ScheduleCountAnnouncement()
    {
        if (!countAnnounced) return;

        announceTimer ??= Time.CreateTimer(
            _ => _ = InvokeAsync(AnnounceCount), null, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
        announceTimer.Change(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(AnnounceDebounceMs), Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
    }

    /// <remarks>Internal for the same reason as <see cref="ApplyTypedQuery"/>.</remarks>
    internal void AnnounceCount()
    {
        // An empty library has no filter to report on, and "No titles match" over a library with nothing in it
        // would be false: Task 5's sentence says it, once.
        if (disposed || !ShowFilters) return;

        var sentence = CountSentence(shown.Count);
        Announcer.Status(sentence);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Singular, plural and zero each have their own form: the obvious literal says "1 titles match." at one.
    /// The number follows the BROWSER'S culture, exactly as the grid's head does -- one number, one grouping, in
    /// one block of text -- and is never formatted invariantly.
    /// </summary>
    internal static string CountSentence(int count) => count switch
    {
        0 => NoMatch,
        1 => "1 title matches.",
        var n => $"{n.ToString("N0")} titles match.",
    };

    private static void Stop(ITimer? timer) => timer?.Change(Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);

    /// <summary>
    /// Moves focus to <paramref name="element"/> unless the user has moved focus themselves, and says
    /// whether it happened.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The guard cannot be written in C#: there is no way to read <c>document.activeElement</c> from Blazor,
    /// and <c>ElementReference.FocusAsync()</c> focuses unconditionally. index.html defines the helper and
    /// says why the move is a conformance route rather than a workaround.
    /// </remarks>
    private Task<bool> FocusIfUnmoved(ElementReference element) =>
        TryFocus(() => Js.InvokeAsync<bool>("movieLibrary.focusIfUnmoved", element, PageFocus.Keepers).AsTask());

    /// <summary>Moves focus to <paramref name="element"/> whatever holds it now.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The successful retry only: the button the user was standing on has gone, so focus would otherwise drop
    /// to <c>&lt;body&gt;</c>. Nothing to ask the helper here -- the control that had focus no longer exists.
    /// </remarks>
    private static Task<bool> FocusOutright(ElementReference element) =>
        TryFocus(async () => { await element.FocusAsync(); return true; });

    /// <summary>Runs one focus move and says whether it happened, swallowing the ways interop can fail.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// BOTH focus moves on this page go through here, and that is the point of extracting it.
    /// <c>ElementReference.FocusAsync()</c> is interop exactly as the helper call is -- it invokes
    /// <c>Blazor._internal.domWrapper.focus</c> -- so an escape from either reveals <c>#blazor-error-ui</c>,
    /// which carries an assertive role of its own, on a page that is already reporting a failure through the
    /// announcer or has just recovered from one: two assertive sources for one event, which is the thing this
    /// project forbids outright. <c>if (disposed) return;</c> upstream narrows the window for the disposal race
    /// but does not close it, which is why <see cref="ObjectDisposedException"/> is in the list rather than
    /// being prevented.
    /// </remarks>
    private static async Task<bool> TryFocus(Func<Task<bool>> move)
    {
        try
        {
            return await move();
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is JSException or TaskCanceledException or ObjectDisposedException)
        {
            return false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Adopts the page the grid asked for, writes it to the address, and says nothing.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// A PRESS ENDS THE COUNT. A facet changed a moment ago has armed the count, and a press inside that window
    /// puts the grid's sentence into the same region: early in the window the visitor hears the page and then a
    /// count for something they did not just do, and in the last settle delay of it the count supersedes the page
    /// sentence while that is still waiting, so the press is never spoken at all. The count is already on screen
    /// in the grid's head and the press is the visitor's latest act, so the count is dropped. Not for a
    /// CORRECTION, though: that raises this too, says nothing, and must not take with it the count of the list
    /// that has just changed under the visitor. The grid makes its correction in the render that carries an
    /// address's page to it, which is exactly <see cref="awaitingGridCorrection"/>.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The grid announces the change itself. R7 wants whoever writes a standing statement to be whoever
    /// clears it, and the grid is what clears the region on its own dispose, so the announce belongs there
    /// too. Announcing here as well would speak the page twice -- and would speak it on the FIRST render,
    /// because the grid raises this for an out-of-range correction as well as for a button press.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The number is adopted EXACTLY as given -- the grid builds its announcement from it, and a page that
    /// clamped, ignored or overrode it would have the grid state a page the visitor is not on. It is also
    /// written to the address, so a deep link to page 99 ends with the address saying page 25.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void OnPageChanged(int value)
    {
        page = value;
        if (!awaitingGridCorrection) Stop(announceTimer);
        syncedWith = QueryState.Canonical(filter, page);
        Address.WritePage(value);
    }

    // ============================================================ the filter

    /// <summary>
    /// Takes the address as the state, unless it is only the echo of a state this page wrote itself.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// The router calls this with the address for EVERY navigation, and a navigation this page started comes
    /// back to it. Adopting that would replace the visitor's search box with the text it held a debounce
    /// earlier. So the canonical text of what was last written is remembered and a match is ignored; a mismatch
    /// is a link followed, or the Back button, and the address wins: the box, the filter and the page all
    /// follow it, and any change pending in the box is dropped, because it was typed against a state that no
    /// longer exists.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The echo can arrive LATE -- after the visitor has typed another letter, or after a second write -- and that
    /// is the case this is built for, because Blazor WebAssembly reports a history change back to .NET by an
    /// asynchronous call from JavaScript. It stays harmless for two reasons: the comparison is with the LAST thing
    /// written, and the router reports the manager's CURRENT address, not the address of the write it is
    /// answering, so a late report of an earlier write reads as the later one. DeferredNavigationManager holds
    /// the report back in the tests to prove both.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void AdoptAddress()
    {
        var fromAddress = QueryState.Read(Q, Conf, Genre, Decade, SortParam);
        var addressPage = QueryState.ReadPage(PageParam);

        var signature = QueryState.Canonical(fromAddress, addressPage);
        if (signature == syncedWith) return;

        var narrowedDifferently = Selection(fromAddress) != Selection(filter);

        syncedWith = signature;
        filter = fromAddress;
        page = addressPage;
        awaitingGridCorrection = true;

        // A search typed against the state this replaces is dropped: it was meant for a list that is no longer
        // on screen. The filter timer needs no stopping for that -- it applies whatever the box holds when it
        // fires, which is now the address's own query, so it finds nothing to change.
        typedQuery = fromAddress.Query;

        if (films.State != CatalogState.Ready) return;

        Refilter();
        if (narrowedDifferently) ScheduleCountAnnouncement();
    }

    /// <summary>A keystroke in the search box: shown at once, applied after the filter debounce.</summary>
    private void OnQueryInput(string text)
    {
        typedQuery = text;

        filterTimer ??= Time.CreateTimer(
            _ => _ = InvokeAsync(ApplyTypedQuery), null, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
        filterTimer.Change(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(FilterDebounceMs), Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Internal so a test can run it the way a timer that had ALREADY queued it would after the page is gone:
    /// disposing the timer stops later firings but not one waiting on the dispatcher, and this would otherwise
    /// write the address a second after the visitor had navigated away.
    /// </remarks>
    internal void ApplyTypedQuery()
    {
        if (disposed) return;

        Apply(filter with { Query = typedQuery.Trim() });
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>A facet or the sort changed. Whatever is in the search box goes with it.</summary>
    private void OnFacetChanged(FilterState next) => Apply(next with { Query = typedQuery.Trim() });

    private async Task ClearAll()
    {
        typedQuery = string.Empty;
        Apply(FilterState.Default with { Sort = filter.Sort });

        // The button that was pressed is removed by the very change it made, and focus on a removed element
        // falls to the body: the next Tab would restart above the skip link. The search box is where the visitor
        // starts again, and it is always there.
        if (panel is not null) await panel.FocusSearchAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The one way a filter changes. Sort comes through here too, although it removes nothing: re-sorting while
    /// on page 12 would otherwise show a different 60 films with no sign of why.
    /// </summary>
    private void Apply(FilterState next)
    {
        if (QueryState.Canonical(next, 1) == QueryState.Canonical(filter, 1)) return;

        var narrowedDifferently = Selection(next) != Selection(filter);

        filter = next;
        page = 1;
        syncedWith = QueryState.Canonical(filter, page);
        Address.WriteFilters(filter);

        Refilter();

        // Only a change to WHICH films are shown is worth a statement. A re-sort leaves the count where it was,
        // and the select announces its own new value; "8 titles match." after it would say nothing new.
        if (narrowedDifferently) ScheduleCountAnnouncement();
    }

    /// <summary>The filter without its ordering: two states with the same selection show the same films.</summary>
    private static string Selection(FilterState state) =>
        QueryState.Canonical(state with { Sort = SortOrder.Title }, 1);

    /// <remarks>
    /// R7: clear what this page set, and raise nothing new. Both calls are cancellations -- they take effect
    /// at once and speak nothing. Without them "The library holds 1,456 films." sits in #status-announcer,
    /// readable in browse mode, long after the films are gone, because the announcer is a scoped singleton
    /// and MainLayout survives navigation; and a message still waiting out its settle delay would be
    /// delivered on the page the user has just arrived at.
    /// <para>
    /// The fetch is deliberately NOT cancelled. See <c>OnInitializedAsync</c>.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        disposed = true;

        // Both debounce timers: one firing after this would write a count into a region the page has just
        // cleared, which is the orphaned standing statement R7 exists to prevent.
        filterTimer?.Dispose();
        announceTimer?.Dispose();

        Announcer.Status("");
        Announcer.Alert("");
    }
}
